1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:04,016
A computer model studies twenty-four dots.

2
00:00:04,336 --> 00:00:08,901
It learns them so well that its
miss is the smallest on the chart.

3
00:00:09,221 --> 00:00:12,357
Then we show it sixty new
dots it has never seen.

4
00:00:12,677 --> 00:00:15,707
Now its miss is almost
ninety times bigger.

5
00:00:16,027 --> 00:00:17,499
So what went wrong?

6
00:00:17,819 --> 00:00:19,760
In five minutes, you will know.

7
00:00:20,080 --> 00:00:23,728
And you will never trust a perfect
score in the same way again.

8
00:00:24,948 --> 00:00:27,551
Start with two children
and a spelling test.

9
00:00:27,871 --> 00:00:31,924
The first child learns the ten words
on the practice sheet, by heart.

10
00:00:32,244 --> 00:00:35,124
The second child learns
how letters make sounds.

11
00:00:35,444 --> 00:00:38,751
On the practice sheet, the
first child scores higher.

12
00:00:39,071 --> 00:00:41,716
On new words, the first child is lost.

13
00:00:42,936 --> 00:00:44,323
Now the computer.

14
00:00:44,643 --> 00:00:46,477
Picture a gentle wave.

15
00:00:46,797 --> 00:00:51,555
We show twenty-four dots near it, each
pushed up or down a little by chance.

16
00:00:51,875 --> 00:00:53,603
That push is called noise.

17
00:00:53,923 --> 00:00:56,184
Sixty more dots are kept hidden.

18
00:00:56,504 --> 00:00:58,339
They are the surprise test.

19
00:00:59,559 --> 00:01:01,969
The model draws a curve through the dots.

20
00:01:02,289 --> 00:01:04,572
It has one dial, called bendiness.

21
00:01:04,892 --> 00:01:07,175
At one, the curve is a straight ruler.

22
00:01:07,495 --> 00:01:12,167
At fourteen, it is like cooked
spaghetti, free to bend almost anywhere.

23
00:01:12,487 --> 00:01:14,620
We score each curve by its miss.

24
00:01:14,940 --> 00:01:19,569
That is how far each dot sits from
the curve, squared, then averaged.

25
00:01:19,889 --> 00:01:21,319
Smaller is better.

26
00:01:22,539 --> 00:01:24,011
Here are the scores.

27
00:01:24,331 --> 00:01:28,149
At bendiness one, the ruler
misses both sets by a lot.

28
00:01:28,469 --> 00:01:30,432
At three, both misses fall.

29
00:01:30,752 --> 00:01:35,851
At five, the hidden miss is zero point
two five, the lowest it ever gets.

30
00:01:36,171 --> 00:01:38,645
At ten, the hidden miss starts to climb.

31
00:01:38,965 --> 00:01:46,603
At fourteen, the studied miss is the best of all, zero
point zero seven, and the hidden miss jumps to six.

32
00:01:47,823 --> 00:01:49,999
Put every bendiness on one chart.

33
00:01:50,319 --> 00:01:53,412
The blue line is the miss
on the studied dots.

34
00:01:53,732 --> 00:01:55,609
It only ever goes down.

35
00:01:55,929 --> 00:01:59,129
The coral line is the
miss on the hidden dots.

36
00:01:59,449 --> 00:02:03,545
It falls, it flattens, and then it
shoots off the top of the chart.

37
00:02:03,865 --> 00:02:07,428
The low point of the coral
line is the model you want.

38
00:02:08,648 --> 00:02:10,205
Why does this happen?

39
00:02:10,525 --> 00:02:13,384
Every dot is part wave and part luck.

40
00:02:13,704 --> 00:02:16,264
A stiff curve can only follow the wave.

41
00:02:16,584 --> 00:02:19,123
A bendy curve follows the luck as well.

42
00:02:19,443 --> 00:02:23,816
But the hidden dots had different luck,
so the swerves point the wrong way.

43
00:02:24,136 --> 00:02:26,013
This is called overfitting.

44
00:02:26,333 --> 00:02:29,619
The model learned the practice
sheet, not the spelling.

45
00:02:29,939 --> 00:02:33,523
The ruler has the opposite
problem, called underfitting.

46
00:02:33,843 --> 00:02:36,211
It is too stiff to follow the wave.

47
00:02:37,431 --> 00:02:39,457
Here is the strangest part.

48
00:02:39,777 --> 00:02:41,313
Take the true wave.

49
00:02:41,633 --> 00:02:45,324
It misses the studied dots by 0.248.

50
00:02:45,644 --> 00:02:50,188
The spaghetti curve misses
them by only 0.069.

51
00:02:50,508 --> 00:02:52,023
It beats the truth.

52
00:02:52,343 --> 00:02:57,100
When a model beats the truth on its own
practice data, it has learned the luck.

53
00:02:58,320 --> 00:03:00,731
Was that one unlucky set of dots?

54
00:03:01,051 --> 00:03:03,077
We pressed New dots ten times.

55
00:03:03,397 --> 00:03:07,195
The best bendiness landed between
four and eight every time.

56
00:03:07,515 --> 00:03:14,149
And bendiness fourteen missed the hidden dots by at
least twice as much as the best one, every time.

57
00:03:15,369 --> 00:03:17,823
So how do you calm a wild model?

58
00:03:18,143 --> 00:03:21,535
Keep bendiness at fourteen,
and watch the hidden miss.

59
00:03:21,855 --> 00:03:25,908
With twenty-four dots to study,
it is six point zero six.

60
00:03:26,228 --> 00:03:32,927
With eighty dots, it falls to zero point one
nine, because more dots leave less room to swerve.

61
00:03:33,247 --> 00:03:39,732
With twenty-four dots and a small penalty on
wild curves, it falls to zero point two six.

62
00:03:40,052 --> 00:03:42,185
The lab calls it the stiffener.

63
00:03:42,505 --> 00:03:48,308
Its real name is ridge, from Arthur
Hoerl and Robert Kennard in 1970.

64
00:03:49,528 --> 00:03:51,491
But the biggest fix is a habit.

65
00:03:51,811 --> 00:03:54,797
Never judge a model on
the data it studied.

66
00:03:55,117 --> 00:03:59,512
Hold some data back, hide it,
and judge the model only on that.

67
00:03:59,832 --> 00:04:04,760
So when someone tells you their model
is almost perfect, ask one question.

68
00:04:05,080 --> 00:04:06,403
Perfect on what?

69
00:04:07,623 --> 00:04:11,889
Here is the same model in the
free Rimi Lab overfitting lab.

70
00:04:12,209 --> 00:04:15,836
At bendiness one, a ruler cuts
straight through the wave.

71
00:04:16,156 --> 00:04:20,977
At five, the curve follows the wave,
and the hidden miss is at its lowest.

72
00:04:21,297 --> 00:04:27,719
At fourteen, the curve swerves to touch every
studied dot, and misses the hidden ones badly.

73
00:04:28,039 --> 00:04:31,516
Add a little stiffener, and
the curve calms down again.

74
00:04:32,736 --> 00:04:34,464
Three things to remember.

75
00:04:34,784 --> 00:04:39,563
One, a near-perfect score on the
data a model studied proves nothing.

76
00:04:39,883 --> 00:04:42,997
Two, judge every model
on data it never saw.

77
00:04:43,317 --> 00:04:48,245
Three, more data, or a small penalty,
calms a model that is too wild.

78
00:04:49,465 --> 00:04:54,436
The same trap waits in sales forecasts,
exam marks and house prices.

79
00:04:54,756 --> 00:04:58,745
Try the overfitting lab for
free at Rimi Lab, link below.

80
00:04:59,065 --> 00:05:02,009
Next time, we head back
out to the night sky.

81
00:05:02,329 --> 00:05:03,460
See you then.

